ucabled for Debian and Ubuntu
=============================

Packages
--------

The source package builds three binary packages:

    ucabled               the system daemon, its systemd unit, the udev
                          rule, the D-Bus system policy and the polkit
                          action
    ucable-agent          the default per-user session agent,
                          ucable-agent.service and the GTK4 prompt
                          helper ucable-agent-helper
    ucable-gnome-agent    the optional GNOME Shell agent extension

ucabled Recommends ucable-agent, so a normal "apt install ucabled" gets
the default agent as well.  ucable-agent and ucable-gnome-agent both
need the daemon and register for its single agent slot, so enable only
one of them per user.

Building
--------

    sudo apt install debhelper cargo rustc pkgconf libgtk-4-dev \
        libgtk4-layer-shell-dev gettext
    dpkg-buildpackage -b -us -uc \
        --hook-preinit=debian/dev-version.sh \
        --hook-done=debian/dev-version.sh
    sudo apt install ../ucabled_*.deb ../ucable-agent_*.deb

A build from a Git checkout that is not a clean release tag is a development
build: the hooks append the committer date and short hash of HEAD to the
version, for example 0.2.0~20261022.gaf437b0, which sorts above earlier
development snapshots and below the 0.2.0 release.  A release tarball has no
.git, so its version stays as packaged.

On GNOME, install the extension package too:

    sudo apt install ../ucable-gnome-agent_*.deb

The build needs cargo 1.92 or newer, because the gtk4 0.11 bindings
require it.  Debian trixie and Ubuntu 24.04 ship older toolchains; build
those with a rustup toolchain instead:

    dpkg-buildpackage -b -us -uc -d \
        --rules-file="debian/rules CARGO=$HOME/.cargo/bin/cargo" \
        --hook-preinit=debian/dev-version.sh \
        --hook-done=debian/dev-version.sh

The -d is needed because the distribution's cargo/rustc do not satisfy the
Build-Depends versions.

Cargo fetches the crates pinned by Cargo.lock from crates.io on the first
build; set CARGO_NET_OFFLINE=1 to build against an already populated
cargo cache without network access.

Installing, upgrading and restarting
------------------------------------

The daemon's postinst reloads the system D-Bus policy and polkit, applies
the udev rule so /dev/uhid gets the ucabled ACL, then starts the daemon
on a fresh install or restarts it on an upgrade.
ucable-agent.service is enabled for all users by debhelper; the agent's
postinst reloads and restarts it in every logged-in session, and a fresh
install also starts it for graphical sessions that are already up, unless
ucable-gnome-agent is installed.

Earlier versions shipped the daemon, the session agent and the GNOME
extension in a single ucabled package.  On upgrade apt installs
ucable-agent automatically through the daemon's Recommends; the GNOME
extension moved to ucable-gnome-agent, so install that package as well
if you used the extension.

Neither maintainer script restarts anything when systemd is not running
(for example inside a container).  On remove, ucabled.prerm stops the
daemon; on purge, debhelper removes the enablement and postrm deletes the
service account and the caBLE identity key.

Bluetooth
---------

caBLE needs a working adapter and bluez's bluetoothd.  The package
depends on bluez but does not start it; the daemon starts regardless and
only the individual transaction fails while Bluetooth is off, matching
the unit's Wants=bluetooth.target.

Snap and Flatpak browsers
-------------------------

Snap browsers reach security keys only through snapd's u2f-devices
interface, which tags devices from a hardcoded USB VID/PID allowlist.
A uhid device has no USB attributes, so /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/
90-ucabled.rules tags the virtual token for the Firefox and Chromium
snaps itself; a browser sandbox that started before that rule was
installed needs a restart.

Flatpak browsers are not covered by udev tags.  This path is untested
here: the sandbox has to expose /dev/hidraw* before the browser can see
the token, for example with

    flatpak override --user --device=all <app-id>

which grants the app access to every device node on the host.

Identity key
------------

The daemon keeps a persistent caBLE identity key in
/var/lib/ucabled/identity.key (created through systemd's StateDirectory=),
reserved for future linking support.  Removing the ucabled package keeps
it; purging deletes it.

Session agent
-------------

ucable-agent.service is a user unit, enabled globally through
/etc/systemd/user/graphical-session.target.wants/.  The package's postinst
starts it for sessions that are already logged in and new sessions start
it at login.  If the agent is not running, start it by hand:

    systemctl --user start ucable-agent.service

On wlroots compositors (Sway, Hyprland) the bundled GTK4 helper can be
presented as a layer-shell overlay by starting the agent with
UCABLED_HELPER_LAYER_SHELL=1, or by passing --layer-shell to
ucable-agent-helper.

GNOME Shell agent
-----------------

ucable-gnome-agent ships /usr/share/gnome-shell/extensions/
ucable-gnome-agent@fuzy.me, a Shell extension that registers as the session
agent itself and shows prompts as shell modal dialogs.  It is installed
system-wide but stays disabled; enable it per user and re-login:

    gnome-extensions enable ucable-gnome-agent@fuzy.me

The daemon has a single agent slot, so disable the user service when the
extension is in use:

    systemctl --user disable --now ucable-agent.service

Removing the ucable-agent package is the cleaner way to make the choice
permanent.  Details and trade-offs: contrib/ucable-gnome-agent/README.md
upstream.
